 % Source: Reviewer A, Question 3.
-\reviewcomment{1.3} How were CrackSQL, RISE, and SEDAR adapted and configured for the four target DBMSs, and how did the authors ensure a fair comparison?
+\reviewquestion{1.3} How were CrackSQL, RISE, and SEDAR adapted and configured for the four target DBMSs, and how did the authors ensure a fair comparison?
 
 \reply
 We have added the configuration of the translation baselines in Section~IV (Evaluation), and the number of initial seeds used for each target DBMS in the experimental setup. Each baseline follows its original translation workflow. SQLGlot and jOOQ use their built-in dialect support; CrackSQL and RISE use the official SQL documentation of each target DBMS as dialect knowledge, while \sedar{} requires no additional dialect knowledge. Since CrackSQL and RISE are designed to translate one query or artifact at a time, we translate the statements of each test case individually and reassemble them in their original order, without schema context. \sedar{} translates complete test cases using the extracted source schemas as context. To ensure a fair comparison, all methods transfer the same collected test files and use the same number of initial seeds for each target DBMS (5,879, 4,551, 5,850, and 5,990 for MonetDB, Virtuoso, DuckDB, and ClickHouse, respectively), and no method receives additional manually written translation rules.
